@database "coding_cli"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: coding_cli.guide 1.1 (02.10.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Coding CLI"
@{h1}Coding CLI

This plugin lets an agent hand a programming task to @{b}Claude Code@{ub}.
Each task becomes a @{i}run@{ui}: one headless Claude Code process, started
on the Claude Code login of the account ScarabHive runs under -- the
operator's subscription, not an API key -- in a fresh git worktree of a
repository the operator listed. What the run changed is committed on a
branch of its own; nothing is merged or pushed. The agent gets back the
branch, the changed files and Claude Code's report -- with a
@{tt}json_schema@{utt} also an object matching it -- and can continue the
same conversation later with @{tt}resume@{utt}.

Runs are locked down (no MCP servers but those the operator names per
workdir, a fixed tool list, no shell unless the operator allows commands),
only listed users may start them, and none starts
while the subscription is near its limit. A long run goes on in the
background; the session that started it is woken when it ends.

The plugin has three tools, no hooks and no panel. It also ships an agent,
@{tt}claude_code_agent@{utt}, that only relays work to Claude Code; the
coder agent has the tools too.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  How a run goes  " link run}
  @{"  What a run may do  " link lockdown}
  @{"  The relay agent  " link agent}

For agents and developers
  @{"  run_task  " link run_task}
  @{"  get_run  " link get_run}
  @{"  cancel_run  " link cancel_run}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Known gaps  " link gaps}
@{body}
@endnode

@node run "How a run goes"
@{h1}How a run goes

@{h2}Starting

@{tt}run_task@{utt} checks, in this order: the user is in
@{tt}allowed_users@{utt}; the task is text of at most
@{tt}max_task_chars@{utt} (20,000) characters; the mode; the
@{tt}json_schema@{utt}, if given; for a resume, that the run is the user's, has ended and left a
conversation and a worktree; the workdir is known; no subscription window
is at or past @{tt}max_window_utilization@{utt}; fewer than
@{tt}max_parallel@{utt} runs are going. Then:

@{bullet}@{tt}git worktree add@{utt} of the repository's HEAD on a new
branch @{tt}coding_cli/<run_id>@{utt}, under
@{tt}data/coding_cli/worktrees/<run_id>@{utt}. A resume reuses its run's
worktree and branch instead.
@{bullet}The workdir's @{tt}exclude@{utt} files are deleted in the worktree
and marked skip-worktree, so the run can neither read them nor commit them
as deleted. So is every other tracked file that holds one of their secret
values (see @{" What a run may do " link lockdown}). The answer lists all
of them under @{tt}hidden@{utt}.
@{bullet}For a workdir with @{tt}mcp_servers@{utt}, an edit run gets its own
MCP config, @{tt}data/coding_cli/runs/<run_id>.mcp.json@{utt} (see
@{" What a run may do " link lockdown}).
@{bullet}Claude Code starts detached (its own process group, no window),
the task on stdin, its stream-json output into
@{tt}data/coding_cli/runs/<run_id>.jsonl@{utt}. Neither Ctrl+C in
ScarabHive's console nor an API restart takes it along.
@{bullet}If anything of this fails, the answer is "not started: ..." and
the worktree and branch made for it are removed again.

@{h2}Waiting

The call waits up to @{tt}wait_s@{utt} (300 s by default) for the end.
Meanwhile the chat shows the run the way it shows a sub-agent: a box under
the call with Claude Code's steps, its text and every tool call with its
arguments and result, and a status line per tool call. The box goes on
after the call has answered, as long as the turn that made the call is
streaming; it is not stored -- after a reload, read the run with
@{tt}get_run@{utt}.

A run that ends in time is answered in full. One still going is answered
with its run id, its latest actions and a @{tt}wake@{utt}: when the run
ends, the session that started it is woken and reads it with
@{tt}get_run@{utt} -- unless it read the end itself already. A run whose
watch ended early (a broken status channel) is answered as still going too:
only an end on disk is answered in full. Stopping the turn before the call
has answered -- while it waits or while its answer goes out -- stops the run
too, and so does the end of the process the call waited in (a crash, a
restart): its caller got no run id and may have gone on without it. A
plugin stopped while the call waits stops the run as well -- unless its
process has ended already, whose own end stands -- and that call answers
with the run as it ended (waiting for a finalize already under way) instead
of failing. Once the
call has answered, only @{tt}cancel_run@{utt} or the time limit stops it.

@{h2}The end

A run is stopped after @{tt}max_run_minutes@{utt} (60). A stop -- time
limit, cancel, stopped turn -- kills Claude Code and everything it started.
Then, once per run, the plugin commits everything left in the worktree as
one commit @{tt}coding_cli <run_id>: <first line of the task>@{utt} (edit
mode only), lists the changes against the base, stores the subscription
usage, the cost and the structured output Claude Code reported and records
the state:

@{table}
State | When
done | Claude Code ended with a success result -- even if a stop came too late
cancelled | stopped (cancel, time limit, stopped turn, a starting call that never answered, the plugin stopped while its call waited) before a success result
failed | an error result, or no result at all (its stderr goes into @{tt}details@{utt})
@{body}

For this commit the plugin's git keeps to the git directory the worktree was
made with and runs no hook and no fsmonitor: the run could have rewritten the
worktree's @{tt}.git@{utt} file or planted a hook.

@{h2}Who watches a run

A run is on disk, not in a process. Its owner -- the plugin instance that
started it -- watches it. When the owner is gone (an API restart, a one-shot
@{tt}agent-cli run@{utt} that ended, a stopped plugin), another instance
takes the run over: at its start and every 30 seconds it looks for such
runs, one instance per dead owner. It stops a run whose starting call
never answered (no @{tt}runs/<id>.answered@{utt}, written right before the
call hands out the run id of a run still going) and watches the
others on, with the time limit of the instance that started the run. Any
@{tt}get_run@{utt}, @{tt}cancel_run@{utt} or @{tt}run_task@{utt} also ends a
run whose owner is gone and whose process has ended, is past its time limit
or never answered. Until its starting call has answered, a run is held by
that call's instance, its watch dead or not: no sweep takes it over -- after
a plugin stop, the call releases it once it has answered. Every instance
looks only at its own data folder.

All instances of the plugin (say @{tt}coding_cli@{utt} for the chat and one
for a production) share that folder, but a run belongs to the instance that
started it (@{tt}instance_name@{utt} in its record): only its runs count
against its @{tt}max_parallel@{utt}, and only its tools reach them. A record
from before the name was kept counts for every instance, and is never
stopped for want of an answer. Shared stay the subscription guard, the
taking over of orphans, and a worktree: a resume is refused while any
instance's run works in it.

@{h2}What stays

Worktrees and branches stay -- @{tt}resume@{utt} needs them. Nothing is
merged. Look at a result with @{tt}git diff <base>..coding_cli/<run_id>@{utt}
(the answer's @{tt}next@{utt} says so); remove a worktree with
@{tt}git worktree remove@{utt} and its branch with @{tt}git branch -D@{utt}
once you merged or dropped it.
@endnode

@node lockdown "What a run may do"
@{h1}What a run may do

@{h2}The command line

@{code}
claude -p --output-format stream-json --verbose --restricted
       --strict-mcp-config --mcp-config data/coding_cli/no_mcp.json
                                       (a workdir with servers: runs/<id>.mcp.json)
       --permission-mode acceptEdits          (plan mode: plan)
       [--allowedTools <Shell>(<cmd>) ... mcp__<server> ... WebSearch WebFetch]
       [--disallowedTools <Shell>(git push:*) ... mcp__<server>__<blocked tool> ...]
       --tools Read,Edit,Write,Glob,Grep[,WebSearch,WebFetch][,<Shell>]
                                       (plan mode: Read,Glob,Grep[,WebSearch,WebFetch])
       [--json-schema <schema>] [--model <model>] [--resume <session>]
       [--append-system-prompt-file CLAUDE.md]
@{body}

@{bullet}The task goes in on stdin: nothing the model writes becomes an
argument but @{tt}json_schema@{utt}. That is an object, checked and passed
as its JSON, one value -- which starts with @{tt}{@{utt} and so is never read
as a flag; NaN and Infinity, which are no JSON, are refused. A @{tt}resume@{utt} passes the session id Claude Code itself
reported, and only if it has the form of one.
@{bullet}No MCP server but the workdir's @{tt}mcp_servers@{utt} (see below);
for any other workdir the MCP config is empty. Nothing else is read: the
user's own connectors never load.
@{bullet}The web only for a workdir with @{tt}web: true@{utt}: Claude
Code's @{tt}WebSearch@{utt} and @{tt}WebFetch@{utt}, named in
@{tt}--tools@{utt} and approved in @{tt}--allowedTools@{utt}. Everything a
page says reaches the model, so such a workdir's runs never get the shell,
and a workdir with both @{tt}web@{utt} and @{tt}mcp_servers@{utt} is
skipped with a warning. Every URL WebFetch calls carries what the model put
in it: what the worktree shows can leave.
@{bullet}The file tools write only inside the worktree; a write elsewhere
is refused and shows up under @{tt}denials@{utt}.
@{bullet}The shell exists only when @{tt}allowed_commands@{utt} is set, and
only in edit mode, never for a @{tt}web@{utt} workdir. It is PowerShell on Windows and Bash elsewhere, and it
runs exactly the listed commands (Claude Code permission patterns such as
@{tt}pytest:*@{utt}). @{tt}git push@{utt}, @{tt}git remote@{utt},
@{tt}git config@{utt} and @{tt}git worktree@{utt} are refused to the shell
tool whatever the list says.
@{bullet}The repository's @{tt}CLAUDE.md@{utt}, which
@{tt}--restricted@{utt} would leave unread, is appended to the system
prompt when the worktree has one.

@{h2}MCP servers

A workdir's @{tt}mcp_servers@{utt} names servers of the AgentSystem MCP
config, @{tt}external_servers.remote_servers@{utt}. Only these load, and
only for that workdir's runs in edit mode; @{tt}--strict-mcp-config@{utt}
keeps the user's own connectors and every other config out.

@{bullet}A name must be a plain one (letters, digits, @{tt}_@{utt},
@{tt}-@{utt}), and its server enabled, with a @{tt}url@{utt} and a
transport the MCP client dials (@{tt}streaming@{utt}, @{tt}http@{utt} and
their aliases, or @{tt}sse@{utt}): Claude Code dials it and starts no
program -- stdio is refused, and so is a misspelt transport. The url may
carry no user, password or query: it goes into the run's MCP file as it is,
and a key in it would land on disk (Claude Code's docs expand
@{tt}${VAR}@{utt} in a url too, unmeasured). Otherwise the workdir is skipped
with a warning when the instance is made.
@{bullet}At the start of each run, a resume too, the plugin writes
@{tt}runs/<run_id>.mcp.json@{utt}: per server its @{tt}type@{utt}
(@{tt}sse@{utt} for an SSE transport, else @{tt}http@{utt}), its
@{tt}url@{utt} and its auth headers as placeholders,
@{tt}{"Authorization": "${CODING_CLI_MCP_0}"}@{utt}. The values -- built
then from the server's @{tt}auth@{utt}, so a rotated token applies -- go
only into Claude Code's environment as @{tt}CODING_CLI_MCP_<n>@{utt}; never
into the file, the command line, the record or the answer.
@{bullet}@{tt}--allowedTools mcp__<server>@{utt} approves the server's
tools: headless, an unapproved call would be refused. @{tt}--tools@{utt}
does not limit MCP tools.
@{bullet}A tool the server's entry blocks (@{tt}tools.blocked@{utt}, which
the MCP client refuses whatever an allowlist says) stays refused:
@{tt}--disallowedTools mcp__<server>__<tool>@{utt}, the name written as
Claude Code writes it (every character but letters, digits, @{tt}_@{utt} and
@{tt}-@{utt} becomes @{tt}_@{utt}). In Claude Code a deny rule wins over
every allow rule. A server's @{tt}tools.allowed@{utt} is read by nobody, the
MCP client included.
@{bullet}Plan mode loads no server: it only reads, and a server's tools may
write.

Measured on Claude Code 2.1.285 (concept M-CC-10) with a bearer token in
the child's environment only: the server came up @{tt}connected@{utt} (so
@{tt}${VAR}@{utt} in a header is expanded from the environment), its 12
tools appeared as @{tt}mcp__<server>__*@{utt} beside Read, Glob, Grep and
StructuredOutput, and a tool call ran headless without a denial.

@{h2}The environment

Claude Code gets an allowlist of environment variables: what it needs to
start and find its login (@{tt}PATH@{utt}, the home and profile folders,
temp, locale, proxies, @{tt}CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR@{utt},
@{tt}CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN@{utt} and the like), plus the names in
@{tt}pass_env@{utt} and the MCP auth values as
@{tt}CODING_CLI_MCP_<n>@{utt}. None of the keys ScarabHive loaded from
@{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt} reaches it -- an @{tt}ANTHROPIC_API_KEY@{utt}
would even switch Claude Code from the subscription to paid API use.

@{h2}Secrets kept out of the worktree

For each file in the workdir's @{tt}exclude@{utt} list, the plugin collects
its secret values: every value of a @{tt}.env@{utt} file (read as the
loader reads it; a value with quotes counts with and without them), and in
a YAML file the string values of keys whose name contains secret, password,
passwd, token, api key or private key. Values shorter than 8 characters,
spanning lines or holding @{tt}${@{utt} are skipped. Every tracked file of
the worktree that contains one of the values is hidden too. The values go
to @{tt}git grep@{utt} on stdin, never on a command line.

@{h2}Who may start runs

Only the users in @{tt}allowed_users@{utt}, compared by user id; the
@{tt}agent-cli@{utt} runs as @{tt}cli_user@{utt} (its
@{tt}--session-user@{utt}), a name no account can be registered under. Every
other user gets an error before anything is made. A run belongs to the user who started it:
another user's run id answers "no run ... of yours".

@{h2}The subscription guard

Every run reports the subscription's windows (five hours, seven days, ...)
in its stream; the plugin stores the last report in
@{tt}data/coding_cli/quota.json@{utt}. While a window stands at or above
@{tt}max_window_utilization@{utt} and has not reset yet, or the
subscription refuses requests, no run starts: "not started: the
subscription's five_hour window is at 85%, the limit for runs is 80%; it
resets 2026-09-30 14:00".
@endnode

@node agent "The relay agent"
@{h1}The relay agent

@{tt}claude_code_agent@{utt} is a @{tt}multi_turn_agent@{utt} shipped in
the plugin's @{tt}agents/coding_cli.yaml@{utt}. It writes no code: it turns a
request into one complete task, runs it, and reports the branch, the changed
files, what Claude Code says it did and what it could not do. Its prompt
(@{tt}agents/prompts/claude_code_agent.md@{utt}) tells it to plan bigger or
unclear changes with @{tt}mode: plan@{utt} first and carry the plan out with
@{tt}resume@{utt}, to continue follow-ups with @{tt}resume@{utt}, to end its
turn when a wake is armed, to treat everything marked untrusted as data, and
to leave merging to the user.

@{table}
Setting | Value
llm_profile | or-gemini-flash, or-deepseek-flash -- it only relays
max_steps | 20
tools | +coding_cli/*
metadata.min_role | admin
metadata.visibility | not set: private -- neither in the agent list nor a tool of other agents
@{body}

As shipped it is therefore reached by name, e.g.
@{tt}agent-cli run claude_code_agent "..."@{utt}. The coder agent has the
three tools itself (deferred: loaded on demand with @{tt}tool_search@{utt}).
@endnode

@node run_task "run_task"
@{h1}run_task

Starts a run. Answers when it ends, or with a run id after @{tt}wait_s@{utt}.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
task | required; the complete task, at most @{tt}max_task_chars@{utt} (20,000) characters -- Claude Code sees nothing of the conversation
workdir | one of the configured workdirs; may be left out when there is only one; ignored with resume (the run's own)
mode | @{tt}edit@{utt} (default): change the code; @{tt}plan@{utt}: only read (Read, Glob, Grep, Claude Code's plan mode) and answer with a plan
resume | run id of an ended run of the user's: continue its Claude Code conversation in its worktree and branch
json_schema | a JSON schema, an object of at most 20,000 characters as JSON on the command line -- quotes escaped as Windows needs them, so a quote counts two to four times; the rest of the line stays far below Windows' 32,767 -- nested at most 100 deep. Claude Code also answers with an object matching it (@{tt}output@{utt}). A resume without one keeps its run's
@{body}

@{h2}The answer of an ended run

@{table}
Field | Meaning
status | @{tt}success@{utt}; the run's own outcome is @{tt}state@{utt}
run_id, mode, workdir | the run
state | done, failed or cancelled (see @{" How a run goes " link run})
branch, base, worktree | the branch, the commit it started from, the worktree folder
commit | the commit the plugin made at the end; null when nothing changed or in plan mode
turns, duration_s | as Claude Code reported them
cost_usd | Claude Code's @{tt}total_cost_usd@{utt}. On the subscription a notional amount -- the measure the stream reports, nothing is billed
abo | subscription usage per window, e.g. @{tt}{"five_hour": 0.34, "seven_day": 0.51}@{utt}
changes | untrusted: @{tt}M<TAB>path@{utt} per file against base, plus @{tt}uncommitted ...@{utt} lines; at most 100 lines and a "... N more"
denials | untrusted: tool calls Claude Code was refused, e.g. @{tt}Write /elsewhere/x.txt@{utt}; at most 20, 120 characters each
result | untrusted: Claude Code's final report, at most 12,000 characters (@{tt}result_cut: true@{utt} when cut)
output | untrusted, only with a @{tt}json_schema@{utt}: the object Claude Code returned for it (@{tt}structured_output@{utt}); dropped, with a note, when its JSON is over @{tt}max_output_chars@{utt} (12,000) characters (characters, not escapes) -- cut, it would match the schema no more -- or nests deeper than 100: Python's own encoder, which writes the record and prints an answer in the agent-cli, gives up at about 1,000. A broken character escape in it, as in every part of the stream, arrives as U+FFFD
hidden | files kept out of the worktree for their secrets; at most 50 and a "... N more"
note | the plugin's own words: why it was stopped, "not committed, the changes are in the worktree", "Claude Code ended without a result, its stderr is in details", ...
details | untrusted: git's error output, the last 600 characters of Claude Code's stderr
next | how to resume, and the @{tt}git diff@{utt} that shows the branch
@{body}

Untrusted fields come as @{tt}{"untrusted": true, "content": ...}@{utt}:
Claude Code read the files it wrote about, so its words are data for the
model, never instructions.

@{h2}The answer of a run still going

@{tt}status: success@{utt}, @{tt}run_id@{utt}, @{tt}state: running@{utt},
@{tt}branch@{utt}, @{tt}running_s@{utt}, @{tt}last_actions@{utt}
(untrusted: the last 12 tool calls and texts, one line of at most 100
characters each), and @{tt}wake@{utt} with @{tt}wake_note@{utt} (see
@{" What the model sees " link model}).

@{h2}Errors

@{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "..."}@{utt}, verbatim:

@{table}
Error | Cause
Claude Code runs on the operator's subscription: this user may not start runs (coding_cli allowed_users) | the user is not listed
task: the complete task as text | task empty or not a string
task: at most <max_task_chars> characters | too long
task: not valid text (a broken character escape) -- send it again | a lone surrogate in the text
mode: edit (change the code) or plan (read and answer with a plan) | another mode
json_schema: a JSON schema as an object, at most 20000 characters as JSON on the command line, its quotes escaped | not an object, too long, nested deeper than 100, or holding NaN or Infinity -- also the one a resume would inherit
resume: no run <id> of yours | unknown run, or another user's
resume: run <id> has not ended yet | still running
resume: run <id> left no conversation or worktree to continue -- start a new run | no Claude Code session, or the worktree was removed
workdir: one of <names> | unknown workdir, or none given with several configured
not started: <reason> | the subscription guard, a git or start failure
not started: <n> run(s) still going (<ids>) -- wait for them or cancel one | @{tt}max_parallel@{utt} reached
resume: another run works in that worktree | a resume of a run whose worktree is in use
@{body}

@{h2}Example

@{code json}
{"task": "In src/util/dates.py, parse_date fails on ISO dates with a Z suffix. Accept it, add a test in tests/util/test_dates.py, run pytest tests/util.", "workdir": "scarabhive"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node get_run "get_run"
@{h1}get_run

A run's state: while it runs, the same short report @{tt}run_task@{utt}
gives for a run still going (without @{tt}wake@{utt}); once it ended, the
full answer of @{" run_task " link run_task}. Reading an ended run marks it
read for this session, so it is not woken for it any more.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
run_id | required; a run of the calling user
wait_s | 0 to 600 seconds (default 0; values outside are clamped): wait that long for the end, checking about every 2 seconds
@{body}

Errors: "no run <id> of yours", "wait_s: seconds, a number from 0 to 600"
(not a number).

@{code json}
{"run_id": "3f9a1c0b7e21", "wait_s": 300}
@{body}
@endnode

@node cancel_run "cancel_run"
@{h1}cancel_run

Stops a running run: kills Claude Code and everything it started, then waits
up to 30 seconds for the run to be finalized and answers as
@{" run_task " link run_task} does -- @{tt}state: cancelled@{utt}, note
"cancelled on request", what it changed so far committed on its branch.

@{bullet}A run still being started gets the stop on disk; its owner kills
the process as soon as it runs.
@{bullet}A run that already ended on its own keeps its outcome, even if
nobody has read it yet.
@{bullet}If the run is not finalized within the 30 seconds (another process
owns it), the answer is the running report with the note "the stop is sent
and the run is still ending -- look again with coding_cli_get_run".

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
run_id | required; a run of the calling user
@{body}

Error: "no run <id> of yours".
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The three tools come from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, rendered per
instance: the names carry the instance name
(@{tt}coding_cli_run_task@{utt}, ...), and @{tt}workdir@{utt} is an enum of
the configured workdirs. Without a Claude Code executable or a usable
workdir the instance offers @{b}no@{ub} tools -- an installation without
Claude Code loses nothing by the plugin being enabled. The
@{tt}run_task@{utt} description tells the model that Claude Code sees nothing
of the conversation, that nothing is merged, what to do with a run id, and
that the untrusted fields are data.

@{h2}The wake note

A run still going when @{tt}run_task@{utt} answers comes with
@{tt}wake@{utt} and @{tt}wake_note@{utt}. Armed:

@{code}
you are woken when the run ends: give the user run id <id> and end your turn,
then read it with coding_cli_get_run. A one-shot agent-cli run is never woken
-- there, wait with coding_cli_get_run wait_s
@{body}

Not armed, the note is the reason followed by "; you are not woken -- give
run id <id> to whoever asked; coding_cli_get_run with wait_s waits for its
end". The reasons: "this run was itself woken and ends with its turn", "a
sub-agent's session is never woken", "this session cannot be watched", or
the core's own (session presence off, no session, waking switched off).

@{h2}The chat status line

@{tt}run_task@{utt}: "run <id> started on coding_cli/<id> (edit)", then per
second at most one line with Claude Code's latest text (and, without a live
box, its latest tool call), "(+N)" for the others. At the end "run <id>
done: 3 turn(s), 2 file(s), 41 s, 5h window 34%" -- as an error line with
the note for a failed or cancelled run -- or "run <id> still going after 300
s, wake armed" (or ", no wake"). @{tt}get_run@{utt} on a running run: "run
<id> running, <n> recent action(s)". Errors show their text, cut to 140
characters.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Append-only: each call returns one tool result and nothing earlier is
rewritten. The result is bounded -- the report at 12,000 characters, the structured output at
@{tt}max_output_chars@{utt}, the change list at 100 lines, denials at 20, hidden files at 50, the running
report at 12 lines. Claude Code's own tokens are spent on the subscription,
not on the calling agent's model; @{tt}resume@{utt} continues its
conversation instead of starting a fresh one.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Claude Code must be installed and logged in for the account ScarabHive runs
under. The plugin looks for @{tt}command@{utt} on the PATH; on Windows,
npm's @{tt}claude.cmd@{utt} is replaced by the @{tt}claude.exe@{utt} it
calls (cmd.exe would parse the arguments by its own rules). psutil is a core
dependency; git must be on the PATH.

The entry ships in the plugin's own @{tt}agents/coding_cli.yaml@{utt}, which
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} includes with every plugin's
@{tt}agents/*.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    coding_cli:
      type: coding_cli
      enabled: true
      description: "Claude Code, headless on the operator's subscription: programming tasks in a git worktree, results on a branch"
      allowed_users: [admin, cli_user]
      workdirs:
        scarabhive:
          path: "."
          exclude: [config/secrets.env, config/local.env, config/local.yaml, config/config.yaml]
      allowed_commands: []
      max_window_utilization: 0.8
      wait_s: 300
      max_run_minutes: 60
      max_parallel: 1
      max_task_chars: 20000
      model: ""
      pass_env: []
@{body}

A workdir that gives its runs MCP servers names them; each must be in
@{tt}external_servers.remote_servers@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
      workdirs:
        scarab:
          path: "../ScarabAnimator"
          mcp_servers: [scarab4]
@{body}

An agent gets the tools with @{tt}+coding_cli/*@{utt} in its
@{tt}agent_config.tools.allowed@{utt}.

@{h2}Settings

All flat in the server entry, read once when the instance is made.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
command | claude | the Claude Code executable, a name on the PATH or a path
workdirs | none | name -> @{tt}{path, exclude}@{utt} or just a path; name of letters, digits, _ and -, at most 40; path relative to the project, must hold @{tt}.git@{utt} -- otherwise skipped with a warning
workdirs.<name>.exclude | none | tracked files a run never sees (see @{" What a run may do " link lockdown})
workdirs.<name>.web | false | true: the workdir's runs search and read the web with Claude Code's @{tt}WebSearch@{utt} and @{tt}WebFetch@{utt}, approved, in either mode, and never get the shell; with @{tt}mcp_servers@{utt} as well the workdir is skipped with a warning; anything else than true: neither
workdirs.<name>.mcp_servers | none | names in @{tt}external_servers.remote_servers@{utt} whose servers the workdir's edit runs load -- enabled, with a url without user, password or query, an HTTP or SSE transport, a plain name; otherwise the workdir is skipped with a warning. The server's @{tt}tools.blocked@{utt} stay refused
allowed_users | none | user ids that may start runs; empty: nobody
allowed_commands | none | shell commands as Claude Code permission patterns; empty: no shell
pass_env | none | environment variable names Claude Code gets beyond its allowlist
model | "" | Claude Code's @{tt}--model@{utt}; empty: the subscription's default
max_window_utilization | 0.8 | no run starts while a window is at or past this share (0.05 to 1)
wait_s | 300 | how long @{tt}run_task@{utt} waits before answering with a run id (0 to 3600)
max_run_minutes | 60 | a run still going after this is stopped (1 to 1440)
max_parallel | 1 | this instance's runs going at the same time, counted over the data folder (1 to 8)
max_task_chars | 20000 | longest task @{tt}run_task@{utt} takes, in characters (1000 to 100000)
max_output_chars | 12000 | longest structured output a run returns, in characters as JSON (1000 to 100000); a longer one is dropped
@{body}

Numbers outside the range are clamped; a value that is no number falls back
to the default.

@{h2}Where data lives

@{tt}data/coding_cli/@{utt} (the data directory), or the folder named by
the environment variable @{tt}CODING_CLI_DATA_ROOT@{utt} (relative to the
project). A second ScarabHive process on the machine -- an app a test starts
-- needs a folder of its own: every instance takes over the runs in its
folder whose owner is gone, and rings their wakes.

@{table}
Path | Holds
runs/<id>.json | the run's record
runs/<id>.task, .jsonl, .err | the task, Claude Code's stream, its stderr
runs/<id>.cancel, .wake, .rung, .final | a stop and its reason, the session to wake, the ring's lease, the finalize claim
runs/<id>.answered | the starting call answered with the run id of a run still going: a process taking the run over lets it go on
quota.json | the last subscription report
runs/<id>.mcp.json | the run's MCP config, for a workdir with @{tt}mcp_servers@{utt}: urls and placeholders, no secret; deleted when the run ends, or when it did not start
no_mcp.json | the empty MCP config
worktrees/<id> | the worktrees; their branches are @{tt}coding_cli/<id>@{utt} in the workdir's repository
@{body}
@endnode

@node gaps "Known gaps"
@{h1}Known gaps

@{bullet}@{b}An allowed command runs code.@{ub} Every entry in
@{tt}allowed_commands@{utt} -- above all a test runner -- executes what the
run wrote, with the operator's rights: it can read any file, secrets
included, and push with the stored git credentials. The deny list keeps only
the shell tool itself from typing git push, remote, config or worktree; a
program an allowed command starts is not checked. A test run in a worktree
also runs that checkout's own @{tt}conftest.py@{utt} -- the comment at
@{tt}allowed_commands@{utt} in @{tt}agents/coding_cli.yaml@{utt} names a
case where that ends the API.
@{bullet}@{b}An allowed command sees the MCP tokens.@{ub} They are in Claude
Code's environment, and every program it starts inherits it; an instance
with both warns when it is made. A credential
in the path of a server's @{tt}url@{utt} (user, password and query are refused) lies
in @{tt}runs/<id>.mcp.json@{utt} while the run goes.
@{bullet}@{b}Secrets are found by value, not by meaning.@{ub} A secret in a
file that is not excluded, one shorter than 8 characters, or one written
differently (split, encoded) stays readable. A task that has to change an
excluded or hidden file is not one for this plugin.
@{bullet}@{b}allowed_users compares names.@{ub} An account named
@{tt}cli_user@{utt} made before that name was reserved passes the check.
@{bullet}@{b}The quota guard is a lower bound.@{ub} It knows the usage as of
the last run; the operator's own Claude Code sessions have used more since.
@{bullet}@{b}A one-shot agent-cli run is never woken:@{ub} its process ends
with its turn. There, @{tt}get_run@{utt} with @{tt}wait_s@{utt} waits.
@{bullet}@{b}Whether a run outlives an API restart depends on how the API
runs:@{ub} a service manager that stops the whole control group (systemd's
default) ends the run with the API; the next look reports it failed.
@{bullet}@{b}What Claude Code leaves running is not stopped.@{ub} A stop
kills Claude Code's whole process tree; a process that is still running
after Claude Code ended on its own is no longer part of that tree.
@{bullet}@{b}max_parallel is no lock across processes:@{ub} two processes
starting a run at the same moment can both start one.
@{bullet}@{b}A ring can come twice:@{ub} when two sweeps take over a dead
ringer's lease at the same instant. The core answers the second wake with
@{tt}being_woken@{utt}, so no second turn starts.
@endnode
